<div class="legal-callout"><strong>Important review notice.</strong> This operational notice describes the current PATH implementation. The DOST VII Data Protection Officer and authorized legal office must approve it, confirm retention periods and lawful bases, and update it before public-portal launch.</div>

<section><h2>1. Who controls the data</h2><p>{{ config('compliance.organization') }} operates {{ config('compliance.system_name') }} and acts as the personal information controller for personal data processed through this system.</p></section>

<section><h2>2. Data PATH processes</h2><ul><li>Account and access data, including names, username, password hash, role, office or organization, province, email address, mobile number, job description, IP address, browser user agent, and session activity.</li><li>Evaluator data, including name, sex, classification, organization, designation, contact details, assignments, evaluation status, and remarks.</li><li>Proposal and proponent data, including proposal title and code, proponent, address, map coordinates, contact person details, sector, province, financial amounts, dates, statuses, documents, comments, and uploaded files.</li><li>Funding, rental, acquisition, liquidation, refund, monitoring, notification, and audit records connected with official proposal administration.</li><li>Technical logs needed to authenticate users, diagnose errors, protect the service, and maintain accountability.</li></ul></section>

<section><h2>3. Purposes and lawful basis</h2><p>PATH uses data to receive, evaluate, approve, fund, monitor, audit, report on, and archive proposals; administer user access; notify assigned or authorized personnel; protect government records; and comply with lawful government duties. Processing should rely on the DOST mandate, applicable law or regulation, performance of public authority or official functions, and other lawful criteria under Sections 12 and 13 of the Data Privacy Act—not on blanket consent where consent is not the correct basis.</p></section>

<section><h2>4. Access and disclosure</h2><p>Data is available only to authorized DOST VII personnel, assigned evaluators, system administrators, auditors, service providers under appropriate safeguards, and public authorities when disclosure is authorized or required by law. Role and geographic data scopes restrict access inside PATH. Users must not export, copy, or disclose records beyond their official authority.</p></section>

<section><h2>5. Map and external services</h2><p>When an authorized user chooses “Find on map,” the typed address is sent by the PATH server to OpenStreetMap’s Nominatim service. Map tiles are loaded from OpenStreetMap, which receives ordinary network metadata such as IP address and browser information. The application also currently loads some fonts, icons, map libraries, and chart libraries from external content-delivery networks. These services are not used by PATH for advertising or behavioral profiling. Their operation is governed by their respective terms and privacy practices.</p></section>

<section><h2>6. Retention and disposal</h2><p>Records must be retained only for as long as necessary for official purposes and according to DOST VII’s approved Agency Records Disposition Schedule, the National Archives of the Philippines requirements, audit rules, litigation holds, and applicable funding or program rules. Public records must not be destroyed solely on a user’s request where law requires their preservation. Authorized and secure disposal procedures apply after the approved retention period.</p></section>

<section><h2>7. Security</h2><p>PATH uses authenticated access, role-based permissions, CSRF protection, password hashing, scoped records, audit activity, input validation, and restricted downloads. DOST VII must complement these controls with HTTPS, secure hosting, backups, vulnerability and patch management, incident response, staff training, access reviews, and appropriate organizational and physical safeguards.</p></section>

<section><h2>8. Your privacy rights</h2><p>Subject to lawful limitations for government and public records, data subjects may request information about processing, access and correction, object where applicable, erasure or blocking where legally available, data portability where applicable, and assistance concerning damages or complaints. Identity and authority may be verified before a request is acted upon.</p></section>

<section><h2>9. Automated decisions and behavioral tracking</h2><p>PATH’s current analytics summarize proposal and program records. They do not track users for advertising and do not make a solely automated decision that determines whether a proposal is approved or funded. Material decisions remain with authorized personnel.</p></section>

<section><h2>10. Contact</h2><address><strong>Data Protection Officer: {{ config('compliance.dpo_name') }}</strong><br>{{ config('compliance.organization') }}<br>{{ config('compliance.office_address') }}<br>Email: <a href="mailto:{{ config('compliance.privacy_email') }}">{{ config('compliance.privacy_email') }}</a><br>Official website: <a href="{{ config('compliance.office_website') }}" rel="external noopener">{{ config('compliance.office_website') }}</a></address><p>Privacy complaints may also be brought to the National Privacy Commission through its official channels.</p></section>

<section><h2>11. Changes</h2><p>This notice must be reviewed when PATH’s purposes, data fields, recipients, service providers, retention rules, or public-facing features change. A separate privacy review and notice update are required before launching the planned public portal.</p></section>
